This strategy seeks to motivating and influencing users through the use of technology by making a desired outcome easier to achieve. Design for Persuasive Technology strategy can exploit and use personal information to provide third party access to information, e.g., a parent, boss or partner who may intervene to punish or recompense behavior.

There are seven types of persuasive technology strategies:

• Reduction (simplification of a procedure);
• Tunnelling (guidance through a procedure, such as a ‘wizard’); 
• Tailoring (individual customisation);
• Suggestion (intervention at the most opportune moment, or kairos);
• Selfmonitoring (allowing users to track their own behaviour);
• Surveillance (allowing others to track users’ behaviour);
• Conditioning (reinforcement).